# Netlify redirects, emitted into the build output by Vite's `public/` passthrough.
#
# Duplicates the static rules in the repo-root netlify.toml on purpose: this file
# travels inside the published folder, so a drag-and-drop deploy — or any deploy that
# does not read netlify.toml — still routes correctly. Netlify merges both, applying
# _redirects first. Keep the two in step.
#
# Rules are matched top to bottom; the first match wins.

# Primary host. The deploy subdomain serves the same files as revifyearth.com, so it
# is sent permanently to the public domain. Matches only this exact host: deploy
# previews (deploy-preview-N--revifyearth...) and branch deploys are unaffected.
https://revifyearth.netlify.app/*  https://revifyearth.com/:splat  301!

# No API server accompanies this deployment. Fail explicitly so the assistant's
# status probe sees a non-OK response and hides the launcher, rather than receiving
# the HTML shell with a 200 and failing later on JSON.parse.
/api/*  /index.html  404

# Build output never falls through to the SPA shell. Real files are served first, so
# this only matches a chunk missing from the current deploy (a tab still running the
# previous build); a 404 lets the app reload once instead of choking on HTML.
/assets/*  /index.html  404

# Real routes. The build writes one HTML file per route (about.html,
# services/report-design.html, ...) with that page's own title, canonical and social
# tags, and replaces the marker below with a rewrite for each one. The list comes
# from src/data/seo.ts and the build fails if it disagrees with the routes in
# src/App.tsx. Netlify matches these with or without a trailing slash, so /about/
# also serves about.html, whose canonical is /about. Do not add rules that add or
# strip trailing slashes: Netlify normalises them before matching, so they loop.
# @seo-routes

# Everything else is a real 404. 404.html is the same app shell with
# `noindex, nofollow` and no canonical; the app renders its NotFound page.
/*  /404.html  404
